<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      Restores the two administration roles to what changeset 036 says they are:
      OWNER holds every permission; TENANT_ADMIN holds every permission except rbac.manage.

      WHY IT DRIFTED, AND WHY ONLY ON SOME DATABASES.
      036 grants both roles by SELECTing from `permissions`, which captures the catalog AS IT
      STANDS THE MOMENT 036 RUNS. Changeset 034 was edited after it had already run on existing
      databases — it now declares pos.order.void.own, which 049 also inserts as a repair — so:

        * a FRESH database declares that code in 034, and 036's SELECT picks it up for both roles;
        * a database that ran the ORIGINAL 034 receives the code from 049 instead, long after 036,
          and 049 grants it to MANAGER and CASHIER only. Neither administration role ever gets it.

      Measured on the live dev auth_db before this changeset (65 permissions in the catalog):
        OWNER 64, TENANT_ADMIN 63, and the single code missing from BOTH is pos.order.void.own.

      WHY IT MATTERED ENOUGH TO REPAIR RATHER THAN NOTE.
      Nothing about voiding actually broke: pos.rego and the controllers allow a void on
      pos.order.void.own OR pos.order.void.any, and both roles hold .any. The damage is to
      anything that REASONS over the grant lattice. 13-07's role catalog will only offer a caller
      a role whose permissions are a subset of their own — the rule that stops a tenant admin
      granting OWNER to an account it controls — and under that rule this one missing row made
      CASHIER and MANAGER unassignable BY OWNER, on the grounds that a cashier can void their own
      order and the owner of the restaurant cannot. Measured live: a TENANT_ADMIN was offered five
      roles instead of seven.

      That is the fail-closed direction, and it is the rule correctly reporting bad data rather
      than the data being fine. Repairing the data is the fix; weakening the rule would not be.

      SHAPE. INSERT ... SELECT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, exactly as 049: a no-op on a fresh
      database where 036 already did this, and a repair on one where it could not. It re-asserts an
      existing intent and grants nothing new — on the dev database it adds precisely two rows, both
      for a code both roles could already exercise through .any.

      rbac.manage stays off TENANT_ADMIN. That exclusion is 13-02's authority split (D-23) and the
      reason a tenant admin is not handed the umbrella permission; re-granting it here would undo
      the split silently, from a changeset nobody would think to look in.
    -->
    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-057-repair-administration-role-grant-drift" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <comment>OWNER = every permission; TENANT_ADMIN = every permission except rbac.manage</comment>

        <sql>
            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code)
            SELECT 'OWNER', code FROM permissions
            ON CONFLICT (role_code, permission_code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code)
            SELECT 'TENANT_ADMIN', code FROM permissions WHERE code &lt;&gt; 'rbac.manage'
            ON CONFLICT (role_code, permission_code) DO NOTHING;
        </sql>

        <!--
          Say so if it did not work, rather than letting a role picker quietly come up short three
          weeks later. Same reasoning as 056's RAISE: an operator should be told what happened, not
          left to deduce it from a missing row.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            DECLARE missing_owner INT; missing_admin INT;
            BEGIN
                SELECT count(*) INTO missing_owner FROM permissions p
                 WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM role_permissions rp
                                    WHERE rp.role_code = 'OWNER' AND rp.permission_code = p.code);
                SELECT count(*) INTO missing_admin FROM permissions p
                 WHERE p.code &lt;&gt; 'rbac.manage'
                   AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM role_permissions rp
                                    WHERE rp.role_code = 'TENANT_ADMIN' AND rp.permission_code = p.code);
                IF missing_owner &gt; 0 OR missing_admin &gt; 0 THEN
                    RAISE EXCEPTION
                      'Administration grant repair did not converge: OWNER is still missing % permission(s) and TENANT_ADMIN % (excluding rbac.manage). Both roles are defined as holding the whole catalogue; anything reasoning over the grant lattice - the 13-07 role catalogue ceiling in particular - will withhold roles because of it.',
                      missing_owner, missing_admin;
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>

        <!--
          No rollback. Reversing this means revoking permissions the changelog says these roles
          hold, and on a fresh database it would revoke rows 036 wrote rather than rows this
          changeset added — there is no record of which is which. Removing granted permissions is
          not a migration's job (054 makes the same call in the opposite direction).
        -->
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
